#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124246 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "e5f16c73-7422-57db-9f63-d1e593b8c3be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5442" :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{b}$. In the affirmative case, find such a vector.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $\\mathbf{T}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{Col}(T)\n$$\n\nIn order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nT\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent.\n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}T \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}T & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10 & 26 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + \\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has a zero row, and so the system is consistent. We have two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable.\nNow, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8x_2-16x_3 = 8 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2=-1-2x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x_1+4(-1-2x_3)+2x_3 = 6 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=5+3x_3\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 + 3x_{3} \\\\ -1 - 2x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting $x_3=0$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "5b6f9536-7ac0-57d6-b5cd-d9ce4f4e2620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5443" :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$?" :question/worked-solution "In general,\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T)\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T$. We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + \\frac{7}{4}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{rank}(T)=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124274 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "372bf9eb-6665-5593-8aab-0fa31f5483d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5619" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$." :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + y \\\\ -x + y \\\\ 2y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 4; - x + y = k; 2y = 8\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4$, which we substitute into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 4 \\\\\nx + 4 &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + y &= k \\\\\n0 + 4 &= k \\\\\nk &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ when $k=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc9988bc-9b5b-58ce-a79e-323a67c970f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123209} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e3768-0ce7-5dd0-a133-d4cc82089ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123210} {:answer/id #uuid "32911fbe-4e94-571b-9a41-936ae98e1b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123211} {:answer/id #uuid "4038ea27-6229-5415-8b0b-dc7d56417901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123212} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba889da-5c0e-527